--   The simulation theorem (round six, `sim`)
-- statement:
--   **The simulation theorem.**
--
--   The following holds (Lean statement, in the scope of module `Solutions.KIL4Sim`):
--
--   ```lean
--   theorem NLogN.KIL.RSim.sim {π : PMap} (hπ : PLow π) {p p' : RS} (h : RSim π p p') :
--       (∀ i v, π i = some v → i ∉ p.writes) → ∀ s, PV π s → p.ok s →
--         p'.ok s ∧ p'.run s = p.run s ∧ p'.cost s ≤ 200 * p.cost s
--   ```
--
--   **Context.** KIL4: literal-to-register simulation of register programs
--
--   The KIL1–KIL3 programs carry the sizes of a configuration (field sizes, volumes, the modulus,
--   pass counts, …) as literals `Ex.lit v`.  A uniform program reads them from parameter stacks
--   instead.  `RSim π p p'` says that `p'` is `p` with some literal occurrences `.lit v` replaced by
--   register reads `.reg i`, where the parameter map `π` says `π i = some v`.
--
--   `RSim.sim`: on a state where every parameter stack `i` holds `v` on top (`PV π s`) and is not
--   written by `p`, the program `p'` is executable whenever `p` is, ends in the same state, and costs at
--   most `200 ×` the cost of `p` (a register read costs at most `200 ×` a literal of the same value).
--   Hence every KIL specification `Sp p s t c` transfers to `Sp p' s t (200 * c)` (`Sp.sim`).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Part of the Lean formalization of the round-six κ-improvement (integer-multiplication-kappa campaign, target `IntMul.Kappa.jain_round6_framework`). The statement is copied verbatim from the Lean development; the platform node carries the same declaration name. Definitions it mentions live in the `IntMul_Kappa_R6_Defs*` bundles (on top of the published κ bundles `IntMul_Kappa_K_Defs*`).
